.TH SYS_PROC_THREADS 2 "2026-05-09" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
sys_proc_threads \- list thread IDs for a process
.SH LIBRARY
System library (libsys, \-lsys)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/sysinfo.h>
.PP
.BI "int sys_proc_threads(pid_t " pid ", tid_t *" tids ", size_t *" count ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sys_proc_threads ()
enumerates the threads belonging to process
.IR pid .
On entry,
.I *count
is the caller's array capacity; on return it is the actual number of
threads the process has.  When the kernel writes more threads than
.I *count
allowed, the array is filled to capacity and the caller is expected
to retry with a larger buffer.
.PP
If
.I pid
is 0, the current process is implied.  Pass
.I tids
=
.B NULL
with
.I *count
= 0 to probe just the count.
.PP
The implementation walks the global scheduler thread list with
.BR sched_iterate_threads (9)
and emits each matching thread's
.B tid
into the user array.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned and
.I *count
holds the true thread count.
On failure, a negated errno is returned.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ESRCH
.I pid
does not refer to an existing process.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I tids
or
.I count
points outside the caller's address space.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_proc_info (2),
.BR sys_proc_list (2),
.BR thr_self (2)
